60,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,Option D relies on vehicles and suppliers. The data shows those are the most fragile links.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Yumi,,Option D is the only one that reaches the outer wards where the bus links are gone.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"Option A has recurring facilitator costs. The grant ends, and the flat budget cannot absorb that.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,May,,Option C uses the existing volunteer infrastructure. The repair clinic already runs without staff supervision.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,Option C is the only one that does not require new staff certifications or overload the roster.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Yumi, I understand the demand, but the winter road closures make D impossible to guarantee.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Ryuji, The attendance sheets show forty visitors a session. We cannot ignore that many people.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Yumi, The vehicle line overran by a third. We cannot fund the fuel for D.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,May,,"@George, Option C has a track record. The old programmes show that volunteer-led models survive.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, I agree. Option C uses the volunteers who are already there. No new training needed.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@George, If we choose C, we avoid the supplier and vehicle risks entirely.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Ryuji, But C only serves the main branch. The outer wards are left behind again.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"@Yumi, The endowment covers the room setup for C, but not the recurring costs of D.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,May,,"@Yumi, The old evening groups in the outer wards were volunteer-led. We can replicate that model.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, That is the key. We can use the existing volunteers without adding to the staff burden.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I am voting for C. It is the only option that does not depend on external logistics.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I am voting for D. It is the only option that reaches the people who have lost their transport.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,I am voting for C. It aligns with the endowment rules and avoids the recurring cost trap.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,May,,I am voting for C. It has a proven track record and uses the existing volunteer infrastructure.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,I am voting for C. It does not require new certifications or overload the staff roster.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
